{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794778894,"nonce":"DCPYEESsn1LXXz/BeFrtP/TqptI=","title":"A hover restyle moves the layout epoch on every scroll frame","message":"Scrolling the cn_demo page under a still pointer changed the layout epoch by 15 on every frame (12 set_style, 3 compute_layout), so `scroll_rows_tree_changed` read the page's paint as stale and every scroll frame was a full paint even though the page qualified for rows.\n\nCause, two parts:\n\n1. `apply_state_styles` reset the node's taffy style to its base and then re-applied the base, hover, active and focus layers one `set_style` at a time. A node whose final layout style equalled the one it had still bumped the epoch on the intermediate sets.\n2. A `w_fit` node's `align_self` is incidental: `resolve_incidental_align_self` drops it in `compute_layout` when the parent names `align-items`. The base taffy style is captured before the first layout, so it still carries `align_self`; each restyle put it back and each layout took it off again. Six nodes on the page ping-ponged every frame.\n\nHow found: `COSTS_ROWS_DIAG=1` in tools/blinc-bench cn_demo_costs prints stale / epoch / on-rows per scroll frame; `#[track_caller]` on `LayoutTree::set_style` and `compute_layout` with a trace of the bumping calls named state.rs (base reset) and tree.rs (incidental align). Release binaries here are stripped, so a backtrace showed nothing.\n\nFix: build the layout style locally and set it once; pass it through `LayoutTree::yield_incidental_align_self` first, the rule `resolve_incidental_align_self` now shares.\n\nHeadless cn_demo, 90 frames scrolling 36 px a frame: before, full 90, render median 4.99 ms, session 0.70 ms. After (with the clip-path rows change for the spinners on the page): 80 on rows, 7 full, 3 declined; render median 0.42 ms, p90 4.92 ms; session 0.13 ms. The layout epoch at the start of the diagnostic dropped from 23123 to 9520, so the ping-pong had been running through every earlier frame of the run as well.","files":null}]}